                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "10101fcd-994f-39a4-8d45-9419ebed3a83",
                        "entry_id": "c5dd0a2f-cf39-3fa2-878d-ffd069b46c6e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Alternative form of Mohe."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "alt-of",
                            "alternative"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "29063ae2-348d-436b-9135-04704c93eb2c",
                                "sense_id": "10101fcd-994f-39a4-8d45-9419ebed3a83",
                                "text": "The most important gold-mine in Manchuria is the Moho mine, on the upper Amur."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "04c7c681-5c75-4d85-a04f-9cd01cb80e23",
                                "sense_id": "10101fcd-994f-39a4-8d45-9419ebed3a83",
                                "text": "Heilungkiang Province.[…]\nThe Chinese have colonised a considerable area about Hulan, reaching to 100 miles north of the Sungari. Here are several prosperous towns, with an export trade similar to that on the south bank. Beyond this area the government is still purely Manchu. The capital is Tsitsihar or Pukwei, around which a settled population is found. Thence to the Amur are a few garrison towns, and in the extreme north are the gold mines of Moho."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "050665c9-91dc-4ec6-8229-009b3890088b",
                                "sense_id": "10101fcd-994f-39a4-8d45-9419ebed3a83",
                                "text": "First, a line N. N. E. will run parallel to the Khingan Range to Khailar, and thence to Moho, the gold district on the right hank of the Amur River. This line is about eight hundred m iles in length."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "148b57cf-c0ac-4a49-b34a-cc004edcde23",
                                "sense_id": "10101fcd-994f-39a4-8d45-9419ebed3a83",
                                "text": "The distance from north to south is about 5,500 kilometres, extending over 49 degrees in latitude from the middle of the Heilungkiang River around Moho to the neighbourhood of the Tsengmu Reef at latitude 4° N.",
                                "translation": "A Concise Geography of China",
                                "english": "A Concise Geography of China"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
